715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147432 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147432 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147432 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147432 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Properties of a Mass-Spring System" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147432 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186147349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147432 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147703 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147703 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147703 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147703 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147703 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147703 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147703 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147703 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147703 #edb/ref 1045 "Simple Harmonic Oscillators" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147703 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186148302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147703 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147703 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "30c5d765-e8a1-5a59-b2db-3126dc6cb354" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148126 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148126 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64217" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148126 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148126 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148126 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "916caca2-b090-554c-9711-817f0993a96f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148126 #edb/ref 1074 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents SHM is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nIn the above case:\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148135 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148135 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64249" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148135 #edb/ref 1071 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 64y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148135 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5e5261a8-ad0c-5d41-be10-3bca647fd461" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148282 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148282 #edb/ref 1061 24273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148282 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fb8af199-a191-5690-bea0-0a35eb912fa0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148282 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148283 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148283 #edb/ref 1061 24274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148283 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c9dc993f-b70d-55f2-a8d7-172387d4dab9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148283 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148284 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148284 #edb/ref 1061 24275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148284 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9576afec-19d2-5298-9645-da309d3031d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148284 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148285 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148285 #edb/ref 1061 24276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148285 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6dc9da9b-7542-5f1e-9694-647b8a7b6b0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148285 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148286 #edb/ref 1061 24277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148286 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a7833f24-dcce-555f-85e0-726ef44e30ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148286 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148302 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148302 #edb/ref 1061 24667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148302 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e5708bf8-87df-5091-87ec-054107d5d443" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148302 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148314 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148314 #edb/ref 1061 25055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148314 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2ce03089-291a-5766-881d-b4df3b3ceeab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148314 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148753 #edb/ref 1064 "**Simple harmonic motion (SHM)** is a special type of periodic motion in which an object oscillates about an equilibrium position. In SHM, the object's acceleration is proportional to the distance from the equilibrium position, and it acts in the opposite direction to the displacement.\n\nIf $y(t)$ represents the position of an object experiencing SHM, then it obeys the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nAssuming $\\omega > 0,$ the general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n- $y = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n- $y =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nFrom the above solution, we see that the **angular frequency** $\\Omega$ equals\n\n$$\n\\Omega = \\omega,\n$$\n\n and the **period** $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\Omega}= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\omega}.\n$$\n\nThe period $T$ represents the number of seconds it takes for the object to complete one full oscillation. Similarly, the **frequency** $f$ (measured in $s^{-1}$) represents the number of times the object oscillates per second and is given by\n\n$$\nf = \\dfrac{1}{T} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{2\\pi}.\n$$\n\nIn forms 1 and 2, the constant $R$ is called the **amplitude**, and it represents the object's maximum distance from the equilibrium position.\n\n**Note:** To see where the SHM equation comes from, remember that the object's acceleration $y''$ is proportional to the distance $y$ from the equilibrium and acts in the opposite direction as the displacement. If the constant of proportionality is $\\omega^2,$ then we have\n\n$$\ny'' = - \\omega^2 y \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad y'' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148753 #edb/ref 1065 4094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148753 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "24f095d6-f256-5975-83c1-db9862ec1dd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148753 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148761 #edb/ref 1064 "A common demonstration of simple harmonic motion (SHM) involves attaching a mass to a spring, pulling the mass some distance away from the equilibrium position, and then letting go so that the mass oscillates back and forth.\n\nIn this setting, we can write the SHM equation in terms of the mass $m$ and the spring constant $k.$ The force on any general object is $my'',$ and in the case of the spring, it must be equal to $-ky.$ So we have\n\n$$\nm y'' = -k y,\n$$\n\nand rearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\ny'' +\\dfrac{k}{m} y = 0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = \\dfrac{k}{m}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148761 #edb/ref 1065 4140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148761 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f62c536d-adfe-50f3-8e40-72cb061b1b27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148761 #edb/ref 1067 "The Equation of Motion for a Mass-Spring System" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330046 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{2}=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330046 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330046 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0b31b196-ffe5-5824-b8f3-ccf950676c54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330792 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330792 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330792 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5eae51ab-60f9-5b49-8f5a-a9fe656ea67f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331040 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{″}+4y=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331040 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331040 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7c2939ae-647d-5a92-a35f-64088b11814b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331122 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+4y=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331122 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331122 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "83cb5cf0-09f0-5fc0-84ab-5c0e35f6e2f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331309 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{″}-4y=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331309 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331309 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "98691ed4-494f-57a5-9b29-f6a26a3937ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332427 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186330046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332427 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186330792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332427 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186331040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332427 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186331122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332427 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186331309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332427 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332427 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332427 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186331040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332427 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7c4f606f-21b0-5945-8625-20858994dddc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332989 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{m}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332989 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332989 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0035afd4-f801-529f-8c26-e89eab48fce0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333257 #edb/ref 1080 "4\\,\\text{m}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333257 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333257 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1db218ae-7fc2-5f61-8143-229541181a57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333765 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\,\\text{m}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333765 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333765 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "550f1c38-acd7-5656-af13-aece8cce4084" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334417 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334417 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334417 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a27ea65a-63dd-575c-8716-61bbc057cee7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334699 #edb/ref 1080 "1\\,\\text{m}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334699 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334699 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c4eb5222-e0ff-5f2a-86d5-d790fe7b0c7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335619 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186332989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335619 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335619 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335619 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335619 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335619 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335619 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335619 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186334417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335619 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ceac7b91-bf3d-5f08-a0dd-74c2347b792d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335692 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335692 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64253" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335692 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335692 #edb/ref 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+64y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $4\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335692 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "525991ab-a7d5-5a16-ae31-a0c0ee48b9e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335692 #edb/ref 1074 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=64\\,⟹\\,\\omega =8.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $4\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=4.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=8R\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n4=8R\\,⟹\\,R=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354689 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354689 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354689 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "040b3a63-19be-5022-9658-3014fb50b885" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355241 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355241 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355241 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "40ec6562-d48f-5a6a-8660-68e1c852de8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355530 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{″}+2y=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355530 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355530 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "616d5349-0070-55c4-b7ca-036db233223d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355830 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355830 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355830 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "84455952-9fa7-5e94-aa0f-d7d5bfb8e11d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356226 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356226 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356226 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b15c462d-1b5e-57a3-adf5-fc61bd9e16cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356416 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{″}+4y=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356416 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356416 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c816f4e0-9064-5352-ac0e-4e0580554bc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356422 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{″}+8y=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356422 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356422 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c8ad12a4-e0d5-5774-a055-04562ef23b35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356520 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}y=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356520 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356520 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d45eee0e-5286-5bae-9aad-b285be2058b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356711 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356711 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356711 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ea5fd280-3e1b-5029-8757-e4ee17ca35c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356895 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356895 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356895 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fe4c80ad-01b9-599e-8626-a6256b045b4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186355241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186355530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357038 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357038 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357038 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186355241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357038 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4bf3659f-43c3-5fd3-9c11-b1a1a81c6eb8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357297 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186354689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357297 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186355830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357297 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357297 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357297 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357297 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357297 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357297 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186356226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357297 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cfbf2f3b-088a-5dda-9944-62e1f201bf5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357712 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357712 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94275" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357712 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357712 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357712 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0b779ea2-64c9-5921-9b3e-0c6d8313380f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357712 #edb/ref 1074 "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nTherefore, only function III could be used to represent $y(t).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357715 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357715 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94285" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357715 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357715 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle of mass $16\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=4\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357715 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ba39584f-74b3-570c-9def-e711c021e6b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357715 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that:\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{4}{16}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394970 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394970 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94274" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394970 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394970 #edb/ref 1071 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+144y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394970 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d55619d9-04e6-5e22-adf5-2ab8249416dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394970 #edb/ref 1074 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=144\\,⟹\\,\\omega =12.\n$$\n\nThe period $T$ is given by:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\omega }\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{12}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394971 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394971 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394971 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394971 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394971 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394971 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394971 #edb/ref 1077 